<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Approximating hedging strategies by deep neural
networks</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key idea that we pursue in this article is to approximate hedging
strategies by neural networks. Before describing this approach in
more detail we recall the definition and approximation properties of
neural networks and prove some basic results on hedging strategies
built from them. While these results show that the approach is
theoretically well-founded, they are only one reason
why we have used neural networks (and not some other
parametric family of functions) to approximate hedging strategies. The
other reason is that optimal hedging strategies built from
neural networks can numerically be calculated very efficiently. This is explained
first for the case of OCE risk measures and for entropic risk. Finally, an extension to
general risk measures is presented.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Universal approximation by neural networks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us first recall the definition of a (feed forward) neural network:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span>
</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
